WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.522
A packet from client to ledger crosses two routers, and nobody on the way knows the whole path.

2
00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:14.895
Each router looks at the destination, picks the next hop from its routing table, and forgets the packet.

3
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:20.174
The simplest table has one line, the default route: everything goes to my gateway.

4
00:00:20.174 --> 00:00:23.848
For router, that sends ledger's packet out to the internet.

5
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:28.820
So a router keeps one line per block of addresses.

6
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:38.297
10.10.4.0/24 means every address whose first 24 bits are 10.10.4, and that block is behind router2.

7
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.000
Now ledger's address matches two lines.

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.473
The longest prefix wins: the line with the most matching bits, because it knows the destination best.

9
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:52.964
So router hands the packet to router2, which does the same, and it reaches ledger.

10
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:54.798
Each router decides alone.

11
00:00:54.798 --> 00:00:59.879
If two routers disagree about a block, they pass a packet back and forth forever.

12
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:03.785
So the IP header carries a counter, the TTL.

13
00:01:03.785 --> 00:01:07.407
Every router lowers it by one before it forwards.

14
00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:10.068
Client sends this packet with TTL 5.

15
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.092
4, 3, 2, 1.

16
00:01:11.092 --> 00:01:21.224
A router may not forward a packet that would arrive with 0, so it drops it, and sends an ICMP time exceeded message back to the sender.

17
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.645
Traceroute uses exactly this.

18
00:01:23.645 --> 00:01:25.658
Its first probe has TTL 1.

19
00:01:25.658 --> 00:01:29.761
Router drops it and reports back, so hop 1 is router.

20
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:34.305
The next probe has TTL 2 and dies one router further.

21
00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:35.707
Hop 2 is router2.

22
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.935
TTL 3 reaches ledger itself.

23
00:01:37.935 --> 00:01:45.855
Nothing listens on the probe's port, so ledger answers port unreachable, and traceroute knows it has arrived.

24
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:50.151
Every router picks the most specific line and lowers the TTL.

25
00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:53.632
Traceroute reads the path from the packets that die.
